<!--META-DATA
{
	tags: vector,
}
-->

<statement>
	<p>
		Given a list of numbers, this program returns the list in ascending order.
	</p>

	<tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
		<col width="12%"/>
		<col width="88%" halign="left"/>
        
		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell>Inputs:</cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>list</pf> (1 x N) double vector — row vector numeric values to be sorted.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>
		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell>Outputs:</cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>list_sorted</pf> (1 x N) double vector — row vector containing the values in <pf>list</pf> in ascending order.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>
		<row>
			<cell>Details:</cell>
			<cell><p>▸ To code this, initialize a vector of zeros to store the output. Use a loop that repeatedly (a) calls your <pf>list_min</pf> function, (b) adds the value to the output vector and (c) deletes the value from <pf>list</pf></p></cell>
		</row>
		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ Do not use MATLAB's <pf>sort</pf> function.</p></cell>
		</row>
	</tabular>

	<p>
		Examples:
		<program>
			<code>
				sorted = list_sort([-4 0 4 8 -12])
				% returns: sorted = [-12  -4  0  4  8]
			</code>
		</program>
		<program>
			<code>
				sorted = list_sort([8 1 3 4 9 1 -3])
				% returns: sorted = [-3  1  1  3  4  8  9]
			</code>
		</program>
	</p>
</statement>

		<task label="vectors-coding-sortAscending">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write a function <pf>sortAscending()</pf> that takes a vector <pf>v</pf> and returns a new vector containing the same values in ascending order. Do not use MATLAB’s built-in <pf>sort</pf> function. Assume you have already written <pf>myMin()</pf> and <pf>firstIndexOf()</pf>. Use a loop.
				</p>

				<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
					<col width="11%" halign="right" />
					<col width="15%" halign="left" />
					<col width="28%" halign="center" />
					<col width="46%" halign="left" />
					<row>
						<cell>Inputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>v</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>vector of any length</p></cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell>Outputs:</cell>
						<cell><p><pf>w</pf></p></cell>
						<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
						<cell><p>elements of <pf>v</pf> sorted in ascending order</p></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>

				<sidebyside widths="52% 46%">
					<stack>
						<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							w = sortAscending([-4, 0, 4, 8, -12])
							w = sortAscending([8, 1, 3, 4])
							w = sortAscending([5])
						</program>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							w = [-12, -4, 0, 4, 8]
							w = [1, 3, 4, 8]
							w = [5]
						</program>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>
			</statement>

			<solution component="exercise-solution">
				<p>
					Here is one possible solution. Each pass finds the smallest remaining value, stores it in the output, and removes that occurrence from a temporary working copy.
				</p>
				<program language="matlab">
					function w = sortAscending(v)
						w = zeros(1, length(v));
						temp = v;

						for i = 1:length(v)
							m = myMin(temp);
							idx = firstIndexOf(temp, m);
							w(i) = m;
							temp(idx) = [];
						end
					end
				</program>
			</solution>
		</task>
